---
import PrivacyPage from '../../components/PrivacyPage.astro';
import icon from '../../assets/blimp/icon.png';
---

<PrivacyPage
  name="Blimp"
  icon={icon}
  description="Blimp collects no personal data. It only goes online to search for lyrics, tags or covers when you ask, and to check for a newer version."
  meta="Effective 28 November 2026 · Applies to the Blimp music player for Android and Windows"
>
  <div class="glance">
    <h2>At a glance</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Blimp only uses the internet if you search for lyrics, which sends a song's title, artist, album and length to LRCLIB, or on Windows for missing tags, which sends the same details to iTunes Search and MusicBrainz. Copies downloaded from GitHub or this website also ask GitHub whether a newer version is out each time they open, which sends nothing about you or your music.</li>
      <li>We don't collect, store, sell or share any personal data.</li>
      <li>Everything Blimp keeps, like playlists and listening history, stays on your device.</li>
      <li>The visualizer only reads the sound Blimp is playing. Blimp never records audio.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>What Blimp does</h2>
    <p>Blimp is a music player for the audio files already on your phone or PC. It works offline, apart from an optional lyrics search and, on Windows, an optional search for missing tags, and a check for new versions that you can turn off. There are no accounts, no ads, no analytics and no third-party tracking tools in the app.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>On Android: permissions and why Blimp asks for them</h2>
    <dl class="perm">
      <div><dt>Music and audio</dt><dd>To find and play the songs on your device, and read their details such as title, artist, album art and lyrics.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Microphone</dt><dd>Android requires this permission for its audio visualizer, which Blimp uses to draw bars that move with the music Blimp is playing. Blimp does not record, store or listen to sound from your microphone.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Control audio settings</dt><dd>For the equalizer and volume normalisation.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Keep running in the background</dt><dd>So music keeps playing with the screen off, with controls in the notification and on the lock screen.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Internet</dt><dd>For the lyrics search and the check for a newer version, described below. Nothing else uses it.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>On Windows: what Blimp reads</h2>
    <dl class="perm">
      <div><dt>Your music folder</dt><dd>Blimp reads the audio files in the folder you choose (Music by default) to find your songs and their details, such as title, artist, album art and lyrics. It doesn't read other folders.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Sound for the visualizer</dt><dd>The visualizer reads the sound Blimp itself is playing to draw bars that move with it. On versions of Windows older than Windows 11, Windows can only give it the sound of the whole PC, so other apps' sound moves the bars too. That sound is used only to draw the visualizer, as it plays. It is never recorded or stored. Blimp does not use your microphone.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Media keys and controls</dt><dd>Blimp shows the song that's playing in Windows' media controls, so your keyboard's media keys can control it.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Searching for lyrics</h2>
    <p>Blimp only searches for lyrics online when you ask it to, and shows a notice first. You can search for one song's lyrics from the player's Lyrics tab, or for your whole library by turning on the lyrics finder, which also looks up new songs. You can turn it off in <strong>Settings → Lyrics finder</strong>.</p>
    <p>When you do, Blimp sends the title, artist, album and length of that song, or of each of your songs, to <a href="https://lrclib.net">LRCLIB</a>, a free public lyrics service, to look up their lyrics. Like any website, LRCLIB can see your internet address when Blimp connects to it. Blimp sends nothing else: no account, no listening history, no device identifier and none of your music. LRCLIB is run independently of Blimp, and we have no control over how it handles requests.</p>
    <p>Lyrics it finds are saved on your device. On Windows, you can choose to save them into your music files instead, which stays on your device too. Turning the finder off stops all lookups, and <strong>Remove found lyrics</strong> on the same page deletes them.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Finding missing tags</h2>
    <p>On Windows, <strong>Settings → Find missing tags</strong> can fill in songs' missing tags and covers. It only runs when you ask it to, and shows a notice first. Blimp sends the title, artist, album and length of those songs to Apple's iTunes Search and to <a href="https://musicbrainz.org">MusicBrainz</a>, a free public music database, and downloads covers from Apple and the Cover Art Archive. Like any website, they can see your internet address. Blimp sends nothing else. These services are run independently of Blimp, and we have no control over how they handle requests. What it finds is saved into your music files, on your device.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Discord status, in older versions</h2>
    <p>Blimp 1.7.3 and earlier, on Windows, had a setting, off until turned on, that showed the song playing on your Discord profile: it passed the song's title, artist, album and a link to its cover to the Discord app on your PC, and sent the title and artist to Apple's iTunes Search and MusicBrainz, and checked the Cover Art Archive, to find that cover. Later versions don't have it, and send none of this.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Checking for updates</h2>
    <p>Copies of Blimp downloaded from GitHub or this website ask <a href="https://github.com/BersamaMus/Blimp">GitHub</a> which version is the newest each time they open, and say so when a newer one is out. Only that question is sent: nothing about you, your device or your music. Like any website, GitHub can see your internet address, and it handles requests under its own privacy policy. You can turn this off in <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; data → Check for updates</strong>. Copies from the Google Play Store or the Microsoft Store don't check, as the store updates them.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Information stored on your device</h2>
    <p>To work as a music player, Blimp saves a few things on your phone or PC. None of it leaves the device through Blimp:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your playlists, favourites and current queue</li>
      <li>Listening history, used for Home, your top songs and listening stats</li>
      <li>Search history</li>
      <li>Lyrics found online, if you search for them</li>
      <li>Your settings, such as theme, equalizer and hidden folders</li>
      <li>On Windows, a saved scan of your music folder and shrunken copies of album covers, so Blimp starts quickly, and the window's size and position</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You can pause or clear your listening and search history at any time in <strong>Settings → Privacy &amp; data</strong>. Uninstalling Blimp on Android, or the Microsoft Store version on Windows, deletes all of it. If you installed Blimp on Windows from this website, uninstalling keeps your saved data in your user folder (AppData) in case you reinstall; you can delete it there.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Device backup</h2>
    <p>If you have turned on Android's own backup, Android may include Blimp's settings and history in your device backup. That backup is made by Android and stored in your Google account under Google's privacy policy. Blimp has no access to it. You can turn Android backup off in your phone's settings. Blimp's own backup feature saves a file only where you choose to save it.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Android Auto and sharing</h2>
    <p>If you connect to Android Auto, Blimp shows your music in your car's display through Android. When you choose to share a song, Android's share menu or Windows' share panel sends the file to the app you pick. Both happen only when you use them, and they don't involve searching for lyrics.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Blimp doesn't collect personal information from anyone, including children under 13.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>If this policy changes, the new version will be posted on this page with a new effective date. If Blimp ever starts to collect data or send anything more, this page will say exactly what and why before that version of the app is released.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about this policy can be asked by opening an issue at <a href="https://github.com/BersamaMus/Blimp/issues">github.com/BersamaMus/Blimp/issues</a>.</p>
  </section>
</PrivacyPage>
